# "End" is used both as something to be brought about and as something already existing. The text marks this difference itself in S3 (an end to be effected versus an independently existing end), so it is not a hidden shift.
# P5 moves from abstracting away from ends to be effected to an independently existing end. That move is the argument's explicit step, not an equivocation, so I do not count P5 as a bridge.
